<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!-- Base application theme. -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.Light.DarkActionBar">
        <!-- Customize your theme here. -->
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/colorPrimary</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryDark">@color/colorPrimaryDark</item>
        <item name="colorAccent">@color/colorAccent</item>
    </style>

    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBar" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:background">@null</item>
        <!-- Deliberately NOT set here: android:windowBackground. Pinning it to
             the splash black was tried and reverted. The WebView does not cover
             the whole window - the status-bar strip (top ~100px) and the
             navigation strip are window background - and this theme is DayNight
             precisely so those strips track the system: #FAFAFA with dark status
             icons in light mode, dark with light icons at night. Forcing them
             black leaves the icon colour still following DayNight, so in light
             mode the clock and the signal icons go dark-on-black and vanish
             (measured: strip luma std 10.2 vs 37.0 when correct), and the two
             light palettes in packages/ui/lib/themes.ts get a near-white page
             framed by black bands. The splash's black belongs to the launch
             theme below, not here. -->
    </style>


    <!-- The launch theme. Android 12+ draws the splash itself from these
         attributes; core-splashscreen reproduces the same splash on 24-30.

         Use the UNPREFIXED androidx attribute names, not the android: ones.
         core-splashscreen's values-v31 variant maps each one into its framework
         twin (android:windowSplashScreenBackground = ?windowSplashScreenBackground
         and so on), so one declaration drives both paths - whereas the android:
         names are API 31 and would not compile against minSdk 24.

         What was here before was the Capacitor template's
         `android:background="@drawable/splash"`, which set neither splash
         attribute. Two consequences, both measured on a Galaxy A17:

           - On 12+ the platform fell back to the LAUNCHER icon, upscaling a
             324px raster to the 288dp (810px) splash slot - a 2.5x blow-up, and
             the "grainy and dull" the founder reported. 324 and not 432 because
             a 450dpi screen resolves BOTH mipmap and drawable to the xxhdpi
             bucket: the same matcher that hands this theme an 864px splash_icon
             (288dp x 3) hands the fallback a 324px foreground (108dp x 3).
           - The background fell through to ?android:colorBackground, which is
             DayNight: #17171A in dark mode but #FCFCFF in LIGHT mode, where the
             mark's cream centre nearly vanished before hard-cutting to the
             black app.

         On 24-30 that same line did something different and also wrong.
         android:background is a VIEW attribute, so it never became the window
         background on any version - but the compat splash view inherits it and
         stretches it to the view rect, non-uniformly. Measured on an API 30
         emulator at the same 1080x2340/450dpi: drawable-port-xxhdpi/splash.png
         (960x1600) stretched to 1080x2121, i.e. 1.125x across but 1.326x down.
         What that distorted was not our mark - the file it stretched was the
         Capacitor TEMPLATE's art, a white field carrying a 128x128 blue
         Capacitor logo, which came out 145x171 at (468,1059). So the pre-12
         splash was a competitor's logo on white, stretched out of square. Now
         the mark is drawn from splash_icon at a true 288dp and measures 303x457
         on that emulator - the same undistorted size as the 305x457 on 12+. -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/splash_background</item>
        <!-- ⭐ VECTOR, not the drawable-<dpi>/splash_icon.png rasters.
             MEASURED on a 450dpi Galaxy A17: the bitmap path renders the mark at
             303x455 with an edge energy of 5.72, while a clean resample of the
             SAME source file to the SAME pixel size gives 9.71 — Android's splash
             pipeline destroys ~41% of the edge detail, apparently resampling
             twice (drawable -> internal icon bitmap -> window).
             No raster asset can fix that: simulating the device's bilinear scale
             from xhdpi, xxhdpi AND xxxhdpi all land near 11, roughly double what
             the device actually produces. A vector is rasterised once, directly
             at the target size, so there is no bitmap resample to lose detail in.
             The artwork is unchanged — a faithful vectorisation of the original
             mark, not a redesign (a redesign was tried in build 6 and reverted).
             The splash_icon.png rasters are deliberately LEFT IN PLACE, unused,
             until a device confirms this. -->
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/splash_icon_vector</item>

        <!-- Belt and braces, not load-bearing. This is the attribute
             core-splashscreen reads to setTheme() the activity back to its real
             theme, but Capacitor already does that itself: BridgeActivity's
             onCreate calls setTheme(R.style.AppTheme_NoActionBar) before
             setContentView, long before installSplashScreen() runs - so androidx
             ends up calling setTheme with an id the activity already has, which
             Activity.setTheme discards. Deleting this line was tested on API 30
             and 36 and changed nothing. It stays because it costs nothing and is
             what keeps this working the day Capacitor drops that setTheme. -->
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/AppTheme.NoActionBar</item>

        <!-- Deliberately NOT set: windowSplashScreenIconBackgroundColor. It
             swaps the geometry to the 240dp canvas / 158dp circle pair, which
             would crop 17% off a 288dp-authored icon, and it paints a visible
             disc behind the mark.

             Also deliberately NOT set: android:windowBackground. On API 24-30
             Theme.SplashScreen points it at compat_splash_screen_no_icon_background,
             the layer-list that IS the pre-12 splash; overriding it here would
             delete the splash on every pre-12 device. -->
    </style>
</resources>